.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
.TH BEAMFS-XFSTESTS 1 "2026-10-10" "beamfs-xfstests" "beamfs development tools"

.TP
.B sweep \fR[\fIselection\fR]
Run every test of a selection once and report one verdict per test. With
no argument, or all, what
.B ./check
runs when given nothing: the auto group, generic and shared tests (734 on
the image of 2026-10-02). sweep generic lists tests/generic/[0-9]*.out
instead.
.IP
Since 2.5.0 the sweep compares the node's fsck.beamfs with the one built in
the beamfs tree (BEAMFS_TREE, else ~/git/beamfs) and says so when there is
none to compare with; it was looked for beside this tool's sources, which
for an installed build do not exist, and the check compared nothing.
meta.txt names the node's mkfs.beamfs and fsck.beamfs (path, sha256, -V),
the kernel's last "mounted v5 (... feat=...)" line, which gives the
features of the volumes formatted, and what the tool check compared. The
parity audit of frozen volumes reads region slots out of the region's
payload (IND_PARITY_FEC) and no longer stops on a CRC volume; the summary
of fsck findings follows the wording of fsck 0.1.8; trace and matrix read
fsck's findings again; scenario writes 3808-byte capsules.
.IP
This is what a whole-suite run should be. Passing an empty selection to
.B ./check
runs all 734 tests in one invocation and returns one exit code: on
2026-09-11 that produced, after two and a quarter hours, the single line
"0 pass, 1 fail out of 1 trials" \(em every per-test mechanism this tool
has attached itself to that aggregate and said nothing about any
individual test.
.IP
Enumeration goes through
.B ./check \-n
so the harness's own exclusions, groups and _requires decide what runs.
Each test gets its own trial, its own archive and its own frozen volume
on failure, written as it goes, so a run killed halfway keeps everything
it measured.
.IP
Listing the selection (./check -n on the node) is given the trial
budget, XFSTESTS_TRIAL_TIMEOUT or 1900 seconds, since 2.3.49; it had
the per-test budget of 300 seconds, which the whole suite does not fit
on an emulated aarch64 node, and the sweep ended before its first
test. The domain a sweep reads the console of when the node stops
answering is beamfs-<node>, the node's own; until 2.3.49 it was the
x86 lab's whatever node was named.
.IP
Since 2.3.50 the listing runs detached on the node and is watched from
the station: every minute the sweep says how many lines the harness
has produced and the last one, and it stops the listing after ten
minutes without a new line, or when the budget is spent, naming the
last test resolved and keeping the harness's output under
evidence/listing/. A listing that ends without a test also keeps it.
.IP
Since 2.3.51 the sweep's preparation writes this node's TEST_DEV and
SCRATCH_DEV into the harness's local.config, and the sweep checks the
header of the listing against the configuration before running a
test. The image's local.config names /dev/vdc as the scratch device,
which is x86-01's; on compute01 that is a USB slot of the bench, and
every aarch64 sweep until 2.3.51 had the harness format it at start.
.IP
Since 2.3.52 the detached listing writes under /var/tmp and not to a
/tmp/*.out file: the harness removes /tmp/*.out when check exits, and
the listing of 2.3.50 on x86-01 finished in its first minute and took
its own output with it. 2.3.52 did not build: the comment that said so
spelled the harness's glob and opened a comment that never closed; 2.3.53 is
the same change, compiled.
.TP
.B soak [seconds]
Random 4 KiB O_DIRECT writes on the bare scratch device for the given
time (default 600 s), each block carrying its number, a generation and
a CRC; every write is read back at once, a sample is read again every
30 seconds, and every written block is read at the end. Anything but
the last generation written is reported as zeros, an older generation
(a lost write), another block's contents (a misplaced write) or
garbage, with the region zone of the 1 GiB layout counted apart. The
device must not be mounted. Added after beamfs 0.1.18 saw region
blocks read back identical right after their write and holding zeros
later, with no write in between.
.TP
.B baseline \fR[\fItest\fR] [\fItrials\fR] [\fIrounds\fR]
Run the same code several times over and report the spread.
.IP
Until this is known, no difference between two revisions means anything.
The same commit measured 8 of 10 one morning and 2 of 10 that evening
with nothing changed between them, and three patches were reverted that
day on differences smaller than that. Defaults to generic/464, ten
trials, three rounds; stores the spread so later comparisons are judged
against it.
.IP
The spread is stored in
.I ~/.local/share/beamfs\-xfstests/baseline\-<test>.log
in the same five columns as
.IR bench.log :
commit, test, trials that passed, trials that failed, trials that
returned no verdict, and then the blocks lost by each failing trial in
the order the trials ran, zeros included.
.IP
Since 2.3.5 that line is written from the runs themselves. Before it,
it was rebuilt from their counters, and the rebuild dropped every loss
of zero, sorted what was left out of trial order, wrote a literal 0 in
the column for trials with no verdict, and stamped every series with
the commit of the first. A campaign measured as 30, 0, 40 was stored as
30, 40, and the failing trial that leaked nothing was not in the file
to be seen.
.IP
Since 2.3.7 a failing trial also reports what the checker found
beside the blocks lost: blocks some inode still references while the
bitmap marks them free, and pointers outside the volume. Neither is a
loss and neither enters the losses; both go to
.I state\-<test>.log
as
.B referenced_free
and
.BR out_of_range ,
one line per trial. Before 2.3.7 a trial that failed on hundreds of
the first kind was recorded as having lost nothing.
.IP
Since 2.3.7 the state taken around each trial also carries
.BR mem.orphan_file ,
the file folios on the LRU that no mapping holds, in kB: Active(file)
plus Inactive(file) minus (Cached minus Shmem) minus Buffers. A folio leaves the
mapped count when its file or device drops it and leaves the LRU only
when it is freed, so a figure that grows across trials and never
falls is memory something still holds a reference to. On 2026-09-21
it reached 6.5 GiB of an 8 GiB guest and the allocator stalled on it.
The run report prints it at the start and at the end.
.IP
The file is rewritten whole by each campaign. Copy it before measuring
again if the previous spread is to be compared against.
.TP
.B bench \fItest\fR [\fItrials\fR]
Measure one test's pass rate over repeated trials and compare it to the
last stored run.
.IP
Different question from
.BR sweep :
bench repeats one test to get a rate, sweep runs many tests once to find
out which fail. The verdict is judged against the baseline spread and
declines to call a difference real when the sample cannot carry it.
.TP
.B report
Print the last run: pass rate, failures with their reasons, and how the
nodes divided the work. Reads the history; runs nothing.
.TP
.B history
Print the stored runs in order, oldest first.
.TP
.B compare \fR[\fIn\fR]
Compare the last two stored runs, or the last \fIn\fR.
.TP
.B trace \fItest\fR
Run one test with full-stack tracing: blktrace in the guest and on the
host device, aligned on one clock, plus the kernel function profile.
See
.B XFSTESTS_TRACE
below.
.TP
.B matrix
Vary one condition at a time across a named set, so a difference can be
attributed to the condition that changed.
.TP
.B sweep
Run every test of a selection once, one verdict each.
.sp
A selection is written rather than looped over. Since 2.2.0 the
forms are:
.sp
.RS
.B generic/013
one test;
.B generic/001-014
a range, the width of the digits kept;
.B generic/074,075,083
a list within one family;
.B @leaks
every test that has ever lost a block;
.B @leaks:N
those that lost one in their last N records;
.B @seen
every test the history has recorded.
.RE
.sp
Several forms combine and each test runs once. The named sets come
from this tool's own loss history, which the shell around it could
only ever restate by hand and let go stale.
.TP
.B deploy
Put the newest image and this repository's tools on a node, and
prove they arrived.
.sp
Since 1.2.0
.B deploy
refuses a node that is running a test, as
.B bench
and
.B sweep
always have. It reboots the node, and a deploy run to look at the
display once rebooted one twelve minutes into a thirty-minute test.
.B XFSTESTS_FORCE=1
takes it anyway.
.sp
Since 2.3.20 the link from commits to image is judged on content: the
layer's copy of the module sources against the repository (a difference
means
.B tools/sync-layer.sh
was not run), the layer's copy against the image's date (a later change
means bitbake was not), and the layer's recipes and configs by commit,
the mirror left out. A commit that changed nothing the kernel is built
from no longer breaks the chain; an unsynchronised layer breaks it
without any commit.
.IP
The fsck.beamfs and mkfs.beamfs it pushes follow the machine's
architecture. For a machine of this station's architecture they are
built here, static; for any other they are the ones bitbake built for
that machine, taken from its build tree. Until 2.3.46 every machine got
the station's build, and an aarch64 node carried tools that could not
start.
.IP
Since 2.3.48 the node is ready when it takes a write under /usr/sbin,
where the tools go, not when it answers ssh: a root filesystem that
came up read-only answers like any other, and every step after it then
failed in its own words. When the write fails, deploy prints the
root's line of /proc/mounts and what dmesg said about it, and stops
before pushing anything.
.TP
.B nodes status
Say what each configured node is, right now, and change nothing.
.sp
One ssh round trip per node: kernel, uptime, load, tasks in
uninterruptible sleep, the size and mount state of both devices, which
tools are installed and which version of
.B fsck.beamfs
answers, whether the beamfs tracepoints are armed, the inode cache, what
a previous trial left running, and what
.B deploy
last established -- with whether the node has rebooted since, which
decides if that record still describes this machine.
.sp
It then names what in the reading would spoil a measurement or explain
one: a device still mounted under a checker that reads it raw, tracing
off so a capture records zeroes,
.B DUMP_CORRUPT_FS
at zero so a failure keeps no image, or a node whose own root is beamfs,
whose inode cache therefore counts the root's inodes and says nothing
about the test volumes.
.sp
Since 2.3.0. Safe during a campaign: it starts, kills and mounts
nothing. Exit status is 1 if a node could not be reached, 0 otherwise --
a node with concerns is still a node that answered.
.IP
.B nodes exec
.I command
runs one command on the first configured node through the tool's own
connection and prints its output and exit code as they are, since
2.3.51.
.TP
.B nodes failures
Print, whole, what the runner kept on each node under
.IR /tmp/xfs-failures :
for a test the budget killed, the watcher's last sample before the kill
and the stacks of the tasks in uninterruptible sleep; for a test that
failed, the harness output, the diff and the kernel messages. Since
2.3.17, when the evidence of a killed generic/074 turned out to hold the
fsck of a mounted volume and none of this. The same file is collected
into the evidence as
.I runner.log
and printed in the run's .md. Reads only.
.TP
.B stop
Stop a running campaign and leave the nodes in a state the next one can
start from.
.IP
A node that does not answer ssh at all is reported as such, never as
stopped. With
.B --hard
its console is kept first, through the hypervisor (SysRq w, l and m,
under evidence/wedge-<node>-<time>/ in the data directory), and the
domain is then restarted and waited for. Before 2.3.13 a silent node
read as "nothing left running" and --hard never reached it.
.sp
A
.B sweep
that sees the disk stop with tasks stuck keeps the stacks of those tasks
in the case's
.I stall.txt
(and in the run's .md), then restarts the domain the same way before the
next test, and stops if the node does not come back. Before 2.3.18 it
went on to the next test with the stuck tasks still holding the device,
and mkfs over that device produced a failure the harness had made
itself.
.sp
Since 2.3.28 a test the budget killed is followed by a restart of the
domain, exactly as a wedge is: what the kill leaves in uninterruptible
sleep holds the device, and the preparation of the next test used to
detach the mount lazily and format under it -- generic/650 on
2026-09-23 ran on a device whose previous filesystem was still alive
and was overwritten by its last flush. The preparation now waits up to
a minute for check, fsstress, fsx and xfs_io to die, never detaches a
mount lazily, and refuses the node if a writer or a mount of the
previous test remains. The progress line also counts the test devices
writing as progress, so a test that prints nothing while writing is no
longer shown as STALLED.
.sp
Since 2.3.25 the same
.I stall.txt
is kept when the budget kills a test on a node that still answers: the
tasks the kill leaves in uninterruptible sleep are asked for their whole
stacks, SysRq w is sent and the whole kernel ring since the test began
is kept with it, along with Dirty and Writeback from /proc/meminfo and
the in-flight count of every virtio disk. On 2026-09-22 three tests were
killed at the budget in one sweep and no stack was kept, because the
node had never stopped answering. The watcher that decides "the disk
stopped" also reads both the test and the scratch device since 2.3.25;
before, it read the scratch device alone, and a test on the test device
was watched through a disk it never touched.
.PP
A sweep also stops when
.I /tmp/beamfs-xfstests.stop
appears: it finishes the test it is on, prints the same summary it would
have printed at the end, and says how many tests were never started.
.IP
A run killed outright loses that summary. On 2026-09-11 a sweep was
killed after 214 passes and 8 failures and the only record was the
scrollback: the evidence directories were on disk, but which tests had
run and which had never started was nowhere. The file can be touched
from another terminal, which is what was wanted at the time and what a
signal would not have given without finding the pid.
.IP
A sweep also stops itself when the node refuses preparation three times
running. Seventeen tests once reported the same connection timeout nine
seconds apart, measuring nothing while the list walked itself to the
end.

.SH NAME
beamfs-xfstests \- run the filesystem test suite against beamfs, and reproduce what it finds

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B beamfs-xfstests
.RI [ command ]
.RI [ args ]

.SH DESCRIPTION
Runs the kernel filesystem test suite against beamfs across a lab of
nodes, and reproduces defects the suite reports so they can be traced.
.PP
.B check -g generic
runs the suite in one process with no per-test timeout. A test that
wedges the filesystem takes the whole run with it: on 2026-09-01
generic/285 sat in
.B folio_wait_writeback
for nine hours and the other 127 tests in that batch never started. A
night of machine time produced one result.
.PP
Here every test is its own invocation with its own deadline, results
are appended as they happen, and an interrupted run resumes from what
it had. Work is split across nodes, because a test that takes ten
seconds on hardware takes two to three minutes under emulation.

.SH COMMANDS
.PP
Since 2.3.26
.B deploy
refuses an image older than the layer or the commits it should carry,
naming each file and commit, and pushes nothing. Before, it said "run
bitbake first" for each of them, then sealed and pushed the stale image
and returned success; the sweep that followed refused it for the same
reason.
.B XFSTESTS_FORCE=1
deploys it anyway.
.TP
.B run
Shard the suite across the configured nodes and follow it.

Before any test runs, four things are established, each because its
absence cost a campaign.

A lock in /tmp names the process that owns the lab. Two orchestrators on
one node share a scratch device and a results file: the second wipes
what the first proved and both run tests against the same disk.

The chain step refuses to start when the repository and the Yocto layer
disagree, or when an image newer than the deployed one sits in the build
directory. The kernel compiles the layer's copy, so a fix committed and
not mirrored is simply not in the build \(em and every commit, diff and
addr2line says it is.

The prepare step stops whatever the last campaign left, clears the
verdict file and the kernel ring when the run starts fresh, and waits
for writeback to drain, before preflight judges the node. Preflight only
looks; it used to look at a node still holding the previous run.

Preflight then checks that the tools answering on the node are the ones
this repository built, that nothing is mounted on the devices, and that
the kernel is this build.
Run the suite. The default when no command is given. Results are
appended to the history as each test finishes, so an interrupted run
loses nothing, and a second invocation resumes rather than starting
over.
.TP
.B report
Print the last run: pass rate, failures with their reasons, and how the
nodes divided the work. Reads the history; runs nothing.
.TP
.BI probe " test " [ node ]
Run one test under observation. Samples are collected over ssh while
the node answers, and the serial console is captured from the host for
the whole run, because it keeps working when the network does not and
is the only place a panic appears. Use this on a test that hangs or
takes the node down.
.TP
.B history
List past runs, newest first, with their commit and pass rate.
.TP
.BI compare " tagA " tagB
Show what changed between two runs: tests that started failing, tests
that started passing, and tests whose reason changed. A fix that breaks
something else shows here.
.TP
.BI trace " [hours] " [captures]
Reproduce the block leak under load and keep the evidence. Defaults to
one hour and ten captures. See
.B THE TRACE COMMAND
below.
.TP
.BI analyse " [capture]"
Read a capture and say what happened to the blocks that went missing.
With no argument, the three newest. See
.B READING A CAPTURE
below.
.TP
.BI matrix " [conditions] " [loops]
Vary one condition at a time and see which ones the leak needs. All
conditions and twelve loops by default; conditions are comma-separated.
The conditions are
.BR scratch-only ,
nothing but the scratch volume;
.BR second-beamfs ,
a second beamfs filesystem alongside as xfstests keeps its test volume;
.BR second-ext2 ,
a second filesystem of another type, to tell a second mount from a
second beamfs mount;
.BR second-beamfs-no-scrub ,
as above with the sweep suspended;
and
.BR memory-pressure ,
cache pressure without a second mount.
.IP
Each cell returns the node to the state its condition names rather than
inheriting what the last one left, and reports what the setup actually
achieved: a mount that failed silently would otherwise produce a result
under conditions nobody chose and look exactly like one that worked.

.SH THE TRACE COMMAND
generic/464 loses blocks on roughly half its runs and takes three
minutes per run, so chasing it through the suite yields one answer per
twenty minutes once a build is counted. The load it applies reproduces
the leak on its own in two to nine loops: sixteen processes each
truncating and rewriting a random file, appending to a second and
forcing writeback on a third, over two hundred files.
.PP
.B trace
drives that load directly and stops at the first loop
.BR fsck.beamfs (8)
reports a lost block. It then drains the kernel trace ring and writes
it, with the block list, under
.IR ~/.local/share/beamfs-xfstests/traces/ .
.PP
Two details of the loop are not adjustable, because getting them wrong
measures the wrong thing:
.IP \(bu 3
The filesystem is made once per series of twelve loops, not once per
loop. generic/464 makes one and runs ten loops on it; the volume fills
and blocks get recycled, which is the regime the leak needs.
Reformatting every loop produced 24494 contiguous blocks lost in a
single loop, against the 28 to 1400 the test itself produces.
.IP \(bu 3
Tracing stays on across the unmount. Stopping it beforehand and
concluding from the silence that unmount issued no synchronous
writeback was an artefact of the window, and cost a day.

.SH WHAT THE MATRIX HAS ESTABLISHED
generic/464 fails about three runs in ten while the same load driven
directly ran fifty-one loops clean, so something in the test's
surroundings rather than its load is a condition of the defect.
.PP
.RS
.nf
scratch alone                 51 loops, no leak
scratch + a second beamfs     leaked at loop 5
  + sweep suspended           leaked at loop 10
  + second volume as ext2     leaked at loop 2
.fi
.RE
.PP
Which rules out the sweep, and rules out anything two beamfs mounts
contend over: a second filesystem of any type is enough. What remains is
what a second active filesystem does to the machine -- memory
pressure, inode eviction, a flusher with two superblocks to serve.

.SH READING A CAPTURE
.B analyse
walks the chain for every lost block, which is the same shape every
time: a block is allocated to an inode, a pointer to it is stored into
a parent, the inode is dirtied and eventually written, and fsck walks
that chain from the medium. So the questions are always which inode
owned it, which parent holds the pointer, and whether the pointer went
in before or after the inode was last written.
.PP
That last distinction decides between two different defects. Pointers
stored
.I after
the inode's last write are lost because the inode on disk is older than
its tree. Pointers stored
.I before
it went to disk with the inode, so the parent block is what failed to
be written \(em and reading that parent on the device tells them apart:
0xcd is a block never written at all, as opposed to one written with
stale contents.
.PP
Leaks are grouped by owning inode, because one inode losing thirty
blocks is one defect rather than thirty, and by parent within that,
because several blocks under one parent means the parent is at fault.
.PP
Example output:
.PP
.RS
.nf
inode 150: 4 lost block(s)
  dirtied 90 times, written 1 (0 synchronous), 62 tree stores
  i_indirect at its last write: 33190
  blocks: 26823 27756 27834 33590
  4 pointer(s) in block 33190 (level 1), slots 5 22 39 56
    -- named by i_indirect: read it on the device
  all stored BEFORE the inode's last write:
    the inode went to disk holding them, so the parent is at fault
.fi
.RE

.SS What a capture holds
A capture is a directory named
.IR <epoch>-<NNN> ,
holding:
.TP
.I trace.txt
The kernel ring at the moment of the leak. Contains the beamfs
tracepoints and the writeback ones, one event per line.
.TP
.I lost.txt
The blocks
.B fsck.beamfs
calls used-but-unreferenced, one per line.
.TP
.I inode_table_blk
Where the inode table starts, so the owning inodes can be read off the
device afterwards without the node still being in that state.
.PP
The question a capture answers is which inode owned a lost block and
what happened to that inode. For a block
.I B
in
.IR lost.txt :
.PP
.RS
.nf
grep "beamfs_block_alloc: .*blk=B " trace.txt | tail -1
.fi
.RE
.PP
names the inode. With that inode
.IR I :
.PP
.RS
.nf
grep -c "beamfs_inode_dirty: dev=[^ ]* ino=I "  trace.txt   # times dirtied
grep -c "beamfs_write_inode: dev=[^ ]* ino=I "  trace.txt   # times written
grep    "beamfs_slot_store: dev=[^ ]* ino=I "   trace.txt   # its tree changes
grep    "writeback_.*ino=I "          trace.txt   # what the VFS did
.fi
.RE
.PP
An inode dirtied a thousand times and written once is the shape the
leak takes.
.B beamfs_slot_store
carries the old value as well as the new, so an overwrite of a live
pointer is visible in one line rather than by correlating two.
.PP
To read an owning inode off the device, with
.I T
from
.IR inode_table_blk :
.PP
.RS
.nf
blk=$(( T + (I-1)/32 )); off=$(( ((I-1) % 32) * 128 ))
dd if=/dev/vdc bs=4096 skip=$blk count=1 | od -An -tx8 -j$off -N128
.fi
.RE
.PP
0xcd repeated is a block mkfs wrote and nothing has touched since:
never written, as opposed to written with stale contents. The
distinction matters and is not otherwise visible.

.SS Retention
Captures older than thirty days are removed when
.B trace
starts. A trace is worth keeping only until the conclusion is drawn
from it; the conclusion belongs in Documentation, not in two hundred
megabytes of ring buffer. Set
.B XFSTESTS_TRACE_KEEP_DAYS
to change the age, or move a capture elsewhere to exempt it.

.SS Progress
A loop takes eleven to sixteen seconds. The indicator names the step
the remote loop has reached \(em cleanup, mount, load, unmount, fsck \(em
so what turns is the work rather than a timer: an ssh call that has
hung leaves a timer spinning exactly as it spins during useful work.
Past thirty seconds in one step the refresh slows and the step is
named; past sixty it blinks a hash and says STALLED, which cannot be
mistaken for motion.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-no\-bell
Finish without ringing. Accepted after any command. See
.B THE BELL
below.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XFSTESTS_NODES
The lab, as
.IR name:host:test_dev:scratch_dev ,
comma separated. Required.
.TP
.B XFSTESTS_FSTYP
The filesystem to run against. beamfs unless set.
.IP
The point of being able to change it is differential. This
station has no ECC memory -- dmidecode reports "Error Correction
Type: None" and EDAC reports "No ECC support" -- so a bit
flipped in host memory is silent, and a run that reports no
kernel error proves nothing on its own. The same load, in the
same VM, on the same devices, against a filesystem known to be
sound is what separates a machine that corrupts from a
filesystem that corrupts.
.sp
Since 2.3.25 the preparation step refuses a type the node's kernel does
not list after a modprobe, or one without its mkfs, and says which. A
run past that point would measure xfstests declining to start.
.TP
.B XFSTESTS_MKFS_OPTIONS
Passed to mkfs.beamfs when a node is prepared.
.TP
.B XFSTESTS_CONFIG
Where the settings file lives. Defaults to
.IR ~/.config/beamfs-xfstests/config .
One
.I key = value
per line, # for comments, keys being the variable names without the
prefix and lowercased: nodes, machine, image, poky_dir, timeout,
mkfs_options. Read before the environment and never overriding it, so a
variable set on the command line stays the operator speaking about one
run.
.TP
.B XFSTESTS_KEEP_IMAGE
Whether check keeps a compressed copy of a volume it declared
inconsistent (DUMP_CORRUPT_FS), as it found it, next to the results.
On by default since 2.3.45; set it to 0 to spend no disk on it.
.IP
It was off, and the frozen image of a test device came from a
.B dd
taken after
.B _check_generic_filesystem
had remade that device: generic/013 on 2026-09-26 came back with two
inodes where fsstress had left hundreds, and the one indirect block
fsck counted as damage was nowhere to be measured. With the dump on,
the sweep brings back the copy check kept, re-encoded to zstd when
needed, and reads the live device only when check kept none.
.TP
.B XFSTESTS_TIMEOUT
Seconds a single test may take before it is killed.
.TP
.B XFSTESTS_TRIAL_TIMEOUT
Seconds a test may take in a sweep before the shell kills it: 1900 by
default, flat, whatever the kernel carries; the watcher cuts earlier a
test whose disks have stopped. Raise it for a test that ends near the
ceiling under a probe -- generic/476 took 1751 seconds on 2026-09-23
with nothing attached -- so the kill does not throw the probe's capture
away with the domain. The budget line at the start of a sweep prints
the value in force; before 2.3.29 it printed 1900 regardless. Since
2.5.1 it is also written to meta.txt, as budget=, at the start and the
end of the sweep.
.TP
.B XFSTESTS_KNOWN_FAILURES
The failures a sweep may report and still exit 0. One test per line,
then why it fails and what reproduces it; # starts a comment. A line
with no reason is refused, and its test fails the sweep like any
other. Defaults to tests/known-failures in the beamfs tree
(BEAMFS_TREE, else ~/git/beamfs); without that file, every failure
fails the sweep. Since 2.5.2.
.TP
.B XFSTESTS_SEED
Seed for the load, so two trials of one test do the same work and can be
compared line by line. generic/464 picks its files and write sizes at
random; without a seed a failing trial and a passing one did different
work and comparing them compared the wrong thing.
.TP
.B XFSTESTS_TRACE
Set to 1 to collect blktrace and the kernel function profile around each
trial. Off by default: both move the timing of a race, and a measurement
that changes what it measures is worth nothing.
.sp
Until 0.7.0 this governed only whether the trace was collected:
the node armed every beamfs tracepoint regardless, so a run made
with tracing off still paid for it, and generic/013 was timed at
950 seconds on a node that was tracing throughout. It now decides
whether the node traces at all, and the preparation step prints
the state it actually read back from the node.
.SS Evidence
Each failing trial keeps a directory of pieces: what the checker
said, what the kernel said, the shell trace, the volume itself.
.sp
Until 1.5.0 repeated lines were collapsed before writing, so what
was kept was already a summary.
.BR generic/476 's
fsck.verbose came out eleven lines long, everything the checker
said about the scratch device reduced to one line and a count, and
a defect appearing a thousand times a run could not be investigated
from what remained. Pieces are now stored as they were said, and
collapsed only when a case is read out loud.
Since 2.3.43 the checker runs three times on each unmounted device:
through the block device's page cache, as any reader of /dev/X does;
after blockdev --flushbufs has invalidated that cache; and on a copy of
the device taken with O_DIRECT, past the page cache altogether, since
flushbufs leaves a folio alone while a buffer on it is still held. The
file ends with the three counts of undescribed indirect blocks and a
VERDICT line saying whether the readings agree.
Since 2.3.45 a failing test also freezes the host's side of the
scratch device: the backing file libvirt reports for it, read on the
station with O_DIRECT, kept as scratch-host.img.zst beside the
guest's scratch.img.zst, so the two can be compared block by block.
The configuration file also accepts ssh_key and user since 2.3.45:
the key and the account that open the nodes.
Since 2.3.61 each node keeps its trials under evidence/<node>/. A
trial's directory is named for the test and the trial number, and was
made directly under evidence/, the same name whichever node ran it: a
chain sweeping x86-01 then compute01 had the second sweep overwrite
what the first had kept. Directories written before 2.3.61 stay at the
top of evidence/. Pruning keeps its count of volume images per node.

Since 2.4.0 a sweep tells a test xfstests did not run from one that
passed. check prints "[not run]" for a test that declines and still
counts it in "Passed all 1 tests"; that line was the verdict, and a
sweep saved as PASS every test that declined for want of reflink,
quotas, O_DIRECT or a device-mapper target, and every test it stopped
before reaching. A declined test is now NOTRUN in the saved run, with
the reason check gave; the summary gives four counts (passed, failed,
not run by xfstests, without a verdict) and lists the reasons. A
group in which some tests ran and some declined is a verdict on the
ones that ran.

Each sweep also keeps its own record in sweeps/<tag>/ beside the saved
runs, named for its start: meta.txt, written at the start and again at
the end (this tool's version, the beamfs commit, the node, its kernel
and taint, the beamfs module's srcversion, the sha256 of check, the
devices, the seal); verdicts.txt, one line per test written as the
verdict is reached; the whole output of check for every test; the
saved results; and copies of the archive, trace and digest written to
/tmp. A kernel taint that differs between the start and the end is
reported. The saved run is named for the start of the sweep.

Since 2.4.1 meta.txt names the beamfs module the node runs. beamfs
exposes no srcversion, and the field 2.4.0 asked for came back empty;
the record now carries whether the module is loaded, the file modinfo
resolves and its sha256 (or "(builtin)"), its version and vermagic,
/proc/version, and on this side the sha256 of the kernel image in the
deploy directory.

Since 2.5.1 meta.txt carries budget=, the seconds a test was allowed
(XFSTESTS_TRIAL_TIMEOUT or 1900), and at the end recoveries=, how many
times the sweep recovered the node and after which test. The x86 sweep
of 2026-10-08 ran under the default 1900 s, killed generic/476 at 1883 s
when it had taken 2317 s alone on the same image, and its record said
neither; the node was restarted after the kill, and the taint read at
the end would have come from the fresh boot. After a recovery the end
taint says nothing of the tests before it, and the sweep says so.
.PP
Since 2.5.2 a sweep ends with a verdict on itself, and its exit status
says it: 0 when the verdicts are clean, 4 when they are not, 1 when the
sweep could not run. They are not clean when a test failed and is not
a known failure (see
.BR XFSTESTS_KNOWN_FAILURES ),
when a test has no verdict or was never started, when the kernel was
tainted before the sweep by a machine check, a bad page, a death, a
warning or a soft lockup, when its taint changed during the sweep,
when the node had to be recovered, when lockdep was not on at the
start or switched itself off (debug_locks, in /proc/lockdep_stats),
or when the apparatus failed. meta.txt carries debug_locks= and
kmemleak= at the start and at the end, known_failures= with the path
and the sha256 of the list used, and gate= with its reasons. Until
2.5.2 a sweep exited 0 whatever it found, and nothing stopped a chain,
or a publication, on its verdicts.
.PP
A test passes when check says it passed and the kernel said nothing,
while it ran, that names an incident: BUG:, WARNING:, Oops, call trace,
uncorrectable or corrupt, read case aside, and LOST POINTER as written,
since the tree checker says "no lost pointer seen" at every unmount.
An RS event line counts whatever its uncorrectable= says: with no
injection, a correction during xfstests is one beamfs should not have
had to make. These are the words the shard runner of
.B run
fails a passed test on, and one list holds them for both. check's own
_check_dmesg catches the generic reports, not what beamfs says when it
finds damage; until 2.5.2 sweep and bench took the line "Passed all"
alone. Such a failure gives as its reason "passed by xfstests, but the
kernel said", with the first lines, and keeps its volume like any
other failure.
.PP
The test device is the one the node's configuration names. The
preparation of sweep and bench, the load of trace and the conditions
of matrix formatted and mounted /dev/vdb whatever the node: the test
device of x86-01, and of no other node.
.TP
.B checkpoint
Say whether this repository, the layer, the image and the node still
describe the same code, and stay silent about nothing.
.sp
The chain has five links -- repository to layer, layer to image,
image to node, and the tools pushed alongside -- and every one of
them has broken silently at least once, each time leaving a campaign
measuring something other than what it named. The checks existed,
written where each defect was found, scattered across three modules
and run at different moments;
.B bench
ran none of them.
.sp
Since 2.0.0 they run together before anything is measured, and a
broken link refuses the run.
.B XFSTESTS_FORCE=1
takes it anyway, which is right for a deliberate comparison against
older code and wrong for everything else.
.SS Display
Output is one block rewritten in place, as tall as there is news,
with a spinner that turns while the node is alive. A line is left
behind only once it is permanent: a verdict, a stage that ended, an
error. Everything else is replaced by what comes after it.
.sp
Until 1.1.0 two threads wrote to the screen at once, the indicator
drawing a spinner with a bare carriage return while the main thread
printed over it, so a verdict could read
.BR "volume kept: 6 MiB compressed82s  check" .
One writer now owns the screen. The model lives in the
.B liveblock
crate and is shared with the build scripts.
.sp
Redirected to a file, the transient block is not written at all and
only the permanent lines are kept, so a log reads as a sequence of
events rather than a field of escape sequences.
.TP
.B XFSTESTS_PROFILE
Set to 1 to arm the kernel function profiler and keep its counts
with every case, as
.B function.profile
in the evidence directory. Independent of
.BR XFSTESTS_TRACE :
one answers where the time goes, the other what the disk did, and
asking the first should not cost the second. Before 0.9.0 the
profiler ran only under full tracing and nothing was ever kept, so
every question about a duration was answered by guessing.
.TP
.B XFSTESTS_BPF
The stem of a bpftrace script from
.BR --probes ,
attached to the node for the length of each test under
.B sweep
and, since 2.3.9, of each trial under
.B bench
and
.BR baseline ,
stopped before the checker runs so the capture is the test and not
its verification, and kept in the case directory as
.IR bpf-<name>.txt .
Nothing runs without it: bpftrace instruments the kernel, and a
campaign measuring durations must not carry a probe it did not ask
for. Before 2.3.9 only sweep read it; a bench named with a probe ran
without one and said nothing.
Scripts shipped: heldfolio counts folios leaving the page cache with
buffers still attached (the pool of 2026-09-21, 4 KiB per unreleased
buffer_head); bhbalance counts buffer_head references taken
(__bread_gfp, bdev_getblk, __find_get_block) and released (__brelse,
__bforget) per calling function, so the sb_bread without its brelse is
named by the function that lost it; iowho (2.3.25) counts every bio
submitted for the test and scratch devices by the kernel stack that
submitted it, in bytes and in requests, with a size histogram and a
split by zone of the volume (inode table, bitmap, parity and budget
regions, data), and the allocations made meanwhile -- the instrument
for a volume that received 9 121 MiB for 220 MiB of file data; churn
(2.3.26) counts allocations and frees by stack, freeing site, inode and
minute, slot stores by level with the live pointers they replace or
clear, and writeback ranges with their lengths -- no key per block, so
it survives the 981 771 allocations iowho counted under generic/074;
cpuwho (2.3.27) samples the kernel stack on every CPU at 99 Hz, with
the per-command count that tells idle from busy, and sums the time
tasks spend in uninterruptible sleep by the stack they slept in -- for
the run where the device is no longer the limit and the test is no
faster.
parity (2.3.29) records, for every indirect block, when its parity was
last computed, into which region block and with how many pointers set,
and for every region block when it was last written to the device,
with the frees of blocks the parity tracepoint had named -- read
against the indirect blocks fsck reports as never described, it says
whether the update never went down or the write carried stale bytes.
generic/476 on 2026-09-23 left 105 such blocks after a clean unmount
on a test that passed. Its answer was "stale bytes", for all of them,
46 of them alone in their region; so since 2.3.30 the script also reads
the bytes: at every dirtying of a region block, which of its fourteen
slots are non-zero in the buffer, and at every write of one, the same
mask read from the page the bio carries, plus every read of a region
block from the device -- a second read is a reload over memory. 2.3.30
tested the first 8 bytes of a slot, which are the parity of the first
29 pointers alone and zero for a sparse file; 2.3.31 tests all 256,
each 8-byte word mapped through the interleave on its own, and records
who dirties a region block, by kernel stack. 2.3.31 unrolled those 448
words and overflowed the BPF stack; 2.3.32 loops. Since 2.3.33 the
harness waits for bpftrace to finish printing its maps after the
interrupt, up to three minutes, instead of killing it three seconds
later -- which on 2026-09-23 cut the listing before the two maps the
run was for -- and the script prints those maps first. 2.3.34 keeps
the read timestamps of region blocks, cleared by mistake in 2.3.33, and
notes that the slot masks read by the script disagreed with the
module's own read-back (0.1.13, 0.1.14) and are no longer a witness.
2.3.35 counts every bio whose range covers a region block, whatever
its operation and wherever it starts -- discards, write-zeroes, writes
longer than a block, writes that begin before the zone -- by operation
and by stack: the medium lost slots that the 4 KiB writes counted so
far had carried.
.IP
The script is taken as attached when it has printed a line of its
own, which a script does from
.BR BEGIN .
bpftrace announces "Attaching N probes" before the probes are armed,
and until 2.3.47 that announcement was the signal: a script slow to
arm missed the whole write phase of the test it was meant to watch.
A script that prints nothing in BEGIN is given the full attach
ceiling before the test starts. scripts/undeposited.bt, added in
2.3.47, keeps every allocation, pointer store, parity deposit and
free of the test devices by block, with stacks and times, and
scripts/undeposited.py joins them with the indirect blocks fsck names
on the frozen images.
scripts/nlink.bt, added in 2.3.54, prints every change of a link count
on the test devices with the namespace operation it happened in, every
directory entry added or removed with its result, and the count each
write of an inode carried; scripts/nlink.py replays them for the inodes
whose link count fsck disputes on the frozen images, from the last
mount of their device, and says whether the count and the names parted
in memory or only on the medium.
.IP
Since 2.3.55 the seed of a bench reaches fsstress and fsx as well as
the test (FSSTRESS_AVOID and FSX_AVOID carry -s and -S); until then
they seeded themselves from the clock and two trials did not run the
same load. XFSTESTS_SEED=random draws a new seed for every trial,
prints it and keeps it in the case as seed. A bench no longer writes
into a previous run's case directory: that directory is moved aside
as <case>.prev-<time>. scripts/nlink.py runs fsck with -v, which is
when fsck names the inodes.
.IP
scripts/scrubwb.bt, added in 2.3.56, prints every check of a block by
the scrubber that corrected it, and so wrote its copy back, or found it
uncorrectable, every indirect block the scrubber's walk starts on,
every allocation and free, and every pointer stored into an indirect
block; scripts/scrubwb.py says, for each write-back, whether the block
changed hands between the start of the walk and the write and whether
it held pointers when it was written over.
Since 2.3.57 it prints every check, and every confirming read the
scrubber makes when a subblock does not decode, with the state bits
the shared buffer had at that moment: the confirming read clears the
buffer's uptodate bit and reads the medium into it again, and a buffer
carrying changes not yet written loses them.
Since 2.3.58 it follows beamfs 0.1.23, where the scrubber asks the file
that owns a block before it reads it and again before it writes it
back, and skips it with -ESTALE when the block is no longer that file's:
every check is printed with the owner inode and the logical block,
every write-back and every confirming read of the medium on their own
lines, and scripts/scrubwb.py reports any block the scrubber decoded or
wrote while its last allocation named another inode or after it was
freed. Traces of 0.1.22 and earlier are read as before.
.IP
Since 2.3.59 a bench records with its result the load its trials ran
under: the seed (a number, random, or none when the test could not be
instrumented), the scrubber interval the node reported, and the
bpftrace script attached. A run is compared only with the last run of
the same test under the same load; with none, it is the baseline for
that load. Until then the last run of the test was taken whatever its
load, and two runs of the same commit, one with a fixed seed and one
with random seeds, were called a regression. Runs recorded before
2.3.59 carry no load and are compared with nothing. Pruning leaves the
directories set aside by hand, kept-*, alone, and names every image it
removes.
.IP
Since 2.3.60 the load a bench is compared under includes the node it ran
on: a bench on compute01 is no longer compared with one on x86-01. A
trial that fails before its own fsck gives the harness's own words as its
reason (_check_dmesg, output mismatch) when there are any. What the
kernel said during a trial goes through the channel the indicator
shares, which no longer overwrites the last of those lines. In a sweep
every test without a verdict is listed with its own reason, in the
results as on screen, and one whose budget ran out before the node gave
the command back is recorded as a hang, not as a test that could not
run. A deploy refused for a stale image names bitbake -C rootfs for the
case where bitbake finds nothing to rebuild.
.TP
.B XFSTESTS_BPF_SCRIPTS
Where the scripts are read from. Defaults to the repository's
.I scripts/
when running from it, otherwise
.IR /usr/share/beamfs-xfstests/scripts .
.TP
.B XFSTESTS_SCRUB_MS
Milliseconds the scrubber waits between blocks. 0 parks the
scrubber; empty, the default, leaves the node as it is.
.sp
Written both to
.B /sys/module/beamfs/parameters/scrub_interval_ms
and to every mounted volume. The first is what matters: xfstests
makes and mounts its own scratch device after the preparation step,
so a per-volume setting never reaches the volume actually under
test. 1.4.0 sets the parameter, which the mounts still to come
inherit; before it, a run asking for no scrubbing got none on the
root filesystem and the test device, and the usual pace on the
scratch device that carried the test.
.sp
The root filesystem of the lab node is itself beamfs, so a scrubber
has been sweeping it at ten blocks a second under every measurement
taken so far, each block costing seventeen Reed-Solomon decodes, and
the pace halves again on every correction. Added in 0.8.0 to make
that a condition rather than a constant.
.TP
.B XFSTESTS_TRACE_NS
Set to 1 for nanosecond timestamps in the shell trace, through
.BR "date +%s.%N" .
Costs a fork per traced line \(em 1.7 ms measured on the lab node \(em
against the microsecond EPOCHREALTIME gives for free. Only worth it for a
run that needs it and can afford the distortion.
.TP
.B XFSTESTS_TRACE_KEEP_DAYS
How long trace directories are kept before being pruned.
.TP
.B XFSTESTS_HOST_DEV
Host block device under the VM images, for the host side of the trace.
Defaults to nvme1n1.
.TP
.B XFSTESTS_DOMAIN
libvirt domain of the node, for the counters taken host-side through
virsh. Defaults to beamfs\-\fIname\fR.
.TP
.B XFSTESTS_NO_RESUME
Start a run from scratch instead of resuming what the history holds.
.TP
.B XFSTESTS_HISTORY
Path to the history file. Defaults under XDG_DATA_HOME.
.TP
.B XFSTESTS_LOAD_NO_TEST_MOUNT
Skip mounting TEST_DEV for load generation.
.TP
.B BEAMFS_HEADER_DIR
Directory holding beamfs_format.h, used to measure the superblock field
offsets rather than assume them. Offsets written by hand were wrong three
times in two days, each time in a way that read as a fact: a set feature
flag looked unset, and an RS volume was diagnosed as CRC. Defaults to
~/git/beamfs/tools/fsck.beamfs.
.TP
.B BEAMFS_REPO
.TQ
.B BEAMFS_TREE
Where the kernel module sources live, for the commit a run is attributed
to.
.TP
.B BEAMFS_ARCHIVE
Where campaign archives are written.
.TP
.B BEAMFS_NO_BELL
Set to anything to stop the bell that rings when a long run finishes.
.B report ,
.B history ,
.B compare ,
.B trend ,
.B deploy ,
.B nodes ,
.B checkpoint
and
.B stop
read or prepare rather than measure, and never ring.
See
.B THE BELL
below.

.SH THE BELL
A sweep of the whole suite takes three hours and a single generic/269
takes twenty-two minutes, so a finished run sits in a terminal nobody
is watching. Every command that drives a node rings when it is done,
every four seconds, until Return is pressed.
Since 2.3.36 the script also counts, by kernel stack, every lookup of
a region block by number (__find_get_block, bdev_getblk): the 2.3.35
run showed no bio but the module's own one-block writes touching the
zone, so whatever changes a region's bytes between the update and the
write does it in memory, and has to find the buffer first.
Since 2.3.38 every parity update, block allocation, block free, and
read or write covering the region zone is also printed as it happens,
as an EV line with its timestamp and cpu, so the sequence of events on
one block can be read back in order: the maps keep only the last of
each, and beamfs 0.1.18 showed that what matters is what comes after.
.PP
The sound is compiled into the binary; there is nothing to install or
configure for it. What is needed is a player, and one of these has to
be on PATH:
.PP
.RS
.nf
paplay      pulseaudio-utils, and what pipewire-pulse answers
pw-play     pipewire
aplay       alsa-utils
play        sox
ffplay      ffmpeg
.fi
.RE
.PP
They are tried in that order and the first that starts gets the sound
on its standard input. With none of them installed the run is silent
and nothing else changes: the bell is a convenience and never a reason
a measurement fails or waits.
.PP
It also stays quiet when standard input is not a terminal -- under a
scheduler, or through a pipe -- because there would be nobody to press
Return and the loop would not end. Pass
.B \-\-no\-bell
for a single run, or set
.B BEAMFS_NO_BELL
for a whole shell, which is what to use when one run is chained into
another or when the sound would come out of the wrong machine.
.PP
When this is packaged, one of those players belongs in the
dependencies. A suggests or recommends rather than a requires: the
tool works without one.

.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/beamfs-xfstests/
Run history and captures. On the workstation, not the node: every trace
taken on the night of 2026-09-08 was written to a node's rootfs and
lost when the next image was built \(em 107 reproduced leaks, gone in
one bitbake.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/beamfs-xfstests/traces/
Captures, one directory per leak.
.TP
.I /var/tmp/beamfs-xfstests/archive/
Per-commit results, for
.BR compare .

.SH EXAMPLES
Run the suite on one node:
.PP
.RS
.nf
XFSTESTS_NODES=x86-01:192.168.122.99:vdb:vdc beamfs-xfstests run
.fi
.RE
.PP
See what the last run did, and what changed since the run before:
.PP
.RS
.nf
beamfs-xfstests report
beamfs-xfstests history
beamfs-xfstests compare <older> <newer>
.fi
.RE
.PP
Watch one test that hangs:
.PP
.RS
.nf
beamfs-xfstests probe generic/285 x86-01
.fi
.RE
.PP
Reproduce the leak for two hours, keeping up to ten captures, detached
so the terminal stays free:
.PP
.RS
.nf
nohup env XFSTESTS_NODES=x86-01:192.168.122.99:vdb:vdc \\
  beamfs-xfstests trace 2 10 > /tmp/campaign.log 2>&1 &
tail -f /tmp/campaign.log
.fi
.RE
.PP
Push harder than the test does, to see whether the leak scales with
contention:
.PP
.RS
.nf
XFSTESTS_LOAD_PROCS=32 XFSTESTS_LOAD_FILES=50 \\
  beamfs-xfstests trace 1 5
.fi
.RE

.SH EXIT STATUS
.B sweep
exits 0 when its verdicts are clean, 4 when they are not, and 1 when
it could not run; the reasons are printed at its end and kept in
meta.txt as gate=.
.PP
Every other command exits 0 if it completed, whatever the tests found,
and non-zero if it could not run: no node reachable, no test suite on
the node, no room for the captures.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fsck.beamfs (8),
.BR mkfs.beamfs (8),
.BR trace-cmd (1)
.PP
Documentation/TODO.md in the beamfs tree carries the state of each
open defect and the rules learnt while chasing them.

